<mujoco model="duplo_brick">
  <!-- THE "CUBE" OF THE `cube-in-bowl` RECORDINGS — a FREE slot.

       ⚠ NOT WHAT `so101_tower.family` USES ANY MORE: the family carries the
       PRINTED 25 mm cube (`props/brick.xml`, generated with its print file by
       `tools/soarm/gen_tower_props.py`). This stays as the recorded object,
       a one-line swap in the family for a policy meant to run on the Duplo.

       The recorded object is a green 2x2 DUPLO-size brick: 31.8 x 31.8 mm,
       19.2 mm tall without the studs (the wrist frames show the four
       studs). Modelled as the plain box; the studs are 4.5 mm knobs the
       jaws do not close on.

       ⚠⚠ 3.2 cm IS ABOVE WHAT `cube.xml` SAYS THE SIM JAW CAN HOLD.
       `examples/tasks/task_grasp_feasibility.mojo` measured that a
       commanded-closed grasp holds a 2.4 cm cube and NOT a 3 cm one on the
       stock jaw. The real arm holds this brick in 50 recorded episodes —
       the hollow underside and the studs give the jaws something the sim's
       smooth box does not. RUN THE FEASIBILITY PROBE ON THIS FAMILY BEFORE
       TRAINING ON `so101_tower_cube_in_bowl`; if it holds at 0 placements,
       the sim object is the gap, not the policy
       (`_the_task_was_not_feasible_and_the_curve_could_not_say_so`), and
       the fix is a thinner brick or a jaw-pad geom, decided by measurement.

       ⚠ `slot_geom=` IN THE FAMILY MUST MATCH: bottom_z -0.0096, top_z
       0.0096, h_radius 0.0226 (the half-diagonal, so two bricks never
       overlap at any yaw).

       density 600: a hollow Duplo brick is ~12 g over 19.5 cm³. NO
       `<inertial>` — derived, see cube.xml. -->
  <compiler angle="radian"/>
  <worldbody>
    <body name="brick">
      <freejoint name="free"/>
      <geom name="geom" type="box" size="0.0159 0.0159 0.0096" density="600"
            rgba="0.55 0.85 0.20 1"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
